From d6448e5a732d2e1d6e19f61020697ce1e4129d27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: snarayana <snarayana@student.ethz.ch>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:37:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(crdt): rank a new write above the values it replaces

A replica can hold a value at a priority above its own heads: it has
merged the block that carries the value but not yet walked the branch
below it, so that branch's root is not a head. A node that restarts with
an empty store is in this state until it has pulled the whole history.
addDAGNode gave its writes max head height + 1, so a write could rank
below a value the replica had already merged, and setValue on every
replica dropped it as stale, with no log and no hook.

Rank a new delta above the current priority of every key it writes, the
way a Lamport clock moves past each timestamp it receives. Once a replica
is in sync the current priorities are at most its head height, so this
changes nothing there. Convergence is unaffected: a block's priority is
fixed when the block is created, and the winning value is still a
function of the set of merged blocks.

The regression test restarts a replica with an empty store, lets it merge
the tip of the history while holding back the rest of the walk, and checks
that a write it makes after the merge wins on the other replica. On main
the write lands at height 2 and loses to the LEFT record at height 42.

Refs #73

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
